! ============================================================================
! LSST (Vera C. Rubin Observatory, MPC station X05) astrometric error model
! ============================================================================
!
! PURPOSE
!
! FCCT14, CBM10 and VFCC17 (the three other models bundled with this crate)
! all predate Rubin Observatory first light and therefore carry no entry for
! station X05. Looking up X05 astrometric weights against any of those
! tables falls back to their generic "ALL" catalog-default rule (e.g.
! FCCT14's `ALL: c=cd @ 0.51, 0.40`), which overstates LSST's true
! astrometric uncertainty by roughly an order of magnitude and makes
! `apply_model_errors`'s `max(reported, model)` correction override LSST's
! own (far more precise) reported uncertainties. This file provides a
! dedicated, empirically-derived entry for X05 instead of forcing that
! mismatch onto a literature table sourced from other instruments.
!
! DATA SOURCE
!
! Alerts were pulled from the Fink broker's `diaSource` stream (the LSST
! Alert Production `diaSource` table, as redistributed by Fink), restricted
! to the following fields:
!
!   diaSource.diaSourceId, diaSource.diaObjectId, diaSource.ra,
!   diaSource.dec, diaSource.raErr, diaSource.decErr,
!   diaSource.midpointMjdTai
!
! Coverage: 2025-10-25 to 2026-09-15 (full Fink LSST stream history at the
! time of this analysis). Volume: 16,379,145 diaSource rows, 5,155,306
! distinct diaObjectId, stored as ~163k Spark-partitioned Parquet shards.
!
! METHODOLOGY
!
! `diaObjectId` associates repeated `diaSource` detections that fall within a
! fixed spatial radius of one another on the sky; it carries no information
! on whether the underlying source is static (star, galaxy, AGN, ...) or a
! moving solar-system object, and this dataset has no independent flag to
! tell the two apart — known and as-yet-unrecognised moving objects can both
! be present under some `diaObjectId` groups. In practice a moving object
! rarely re-lands within the association radius across visits separated by
! more than a night or two, so grouping by `diaObjectId` over the full
! ~11-month baseline used here is expected to be dominated by genuinely
! static sources, with a residual minority of contaminated groups. The
! robust rejection step below (point 3) is precisely what keeps such
! contamination from biasing the final RMS: since the true sky position of a
! static source does not change, the scatter of its repeated detections
! around a per-object reference position is a direct empirical measurement
! of the LSST astrometric pipeline's positional error, without requiring any
! external reference catalog or orbit determination — provided groups
! dominated by real motion are identified and excluded rather than averaged
! in.
!
! For every diaObjectId with at least 3 detections:
!
!   1. Two candidate models are fit per axis (RA, projected by cos(dec), and
!      Dec independently), in closed form: a constant-position model (one
!      parameter) and a linear-in-time model (position + rate, two
!      parameters). The linear model absorbs genuine stellar proper motion
!      or systematic centroid drift; it is adopted only when a one-degree-
!      of-freedom F-test rejects the constant model at alpha = 0.01 (and
!      only for objects with >= 4 detections, below which a slope cannot be
!      trusted). 2.4% / 1.9% of eligible objects triggered the linear model
!      on RA / Dec respectively.
!   2. Per-point residuals (observed minus model-predicted position, in
!      arcsec) are computed for every retained detection.
!   3. A robust (MAD-based) 5-sigma iterative clip removes residuals
!      inconsistent with a well-behaved single-source measurement (crowded-
!      field blends, mismatched associations, genuine photometric/
!      astrometric outliers). This removed 23.9% of candidate points —
!      notably higher than a clean single-Gaussian population, indicating a
!      non-negligible fraction of `diaObjectId` groups are not cleanly
!      single, static point sources over a ~1 year baseline (see LIMITATIONS
!      below).
!   4. The final RMS per axis is the root-mean-square of the retained
!      residuals — this is the value recorded below.
!
! RESULTS (arcsec, 2 significant decimals as used throughout this table)
!
!   objects eligible (>=3 detections): 797,114 / 5,155,306 total
!   detections used after clipping: 8,504,533 (2,672,963 clipped, 23.9%)
!   empirical RMS:            ra = 0.0898   dec = 0.0556
!   self-reported (raErr/decErr) median: ra = 0.0350   dec = 0.0350
!   pull (= residual / reported error) robust scale (MAD-sigma):
!                              ra = 3.80     dec = 2.42
!
! The empirical RMS is 2-3x the self-reported median error and the pull
! scale confirms LSST's per-alert `raErr`/`decErr` fields underestimate the
! true positional scatter by a similar factor — so this table intentionally
! does NOT simply reuse the self-reported median, and is far tighter than
! the pre-Rubin literature fallback (FCCT14 ALL: 0.51, 0.40).
!
! LIMITATIONS
!
!   - `diaObjectId` is not a verified-static catalog: known or unrecognised
!     solar-system objects may be present among the groups used here (see
!     METHODOLOGY). Where a moving object's apparent motion across its
!     brief window inside the association radius happens to be well fit by
!     the linear-in-time model, it contributes to the RMS the same way a
!     genuine proper-motion star would — which is harmless for this
!     analysis, since the quantity being measured is the LSST centroiding
!     pipeline's positional *precision*, not the true position of any
!     specific source. This entry was not cross-checked against an
!     independent, confirmed asteroid astrometry sample.
!   - No reference-catalog breakdown is available in the source data. The
!     entry below is keyed on catalog code "c" (rather than a specific
!     catalog letter) because that is the fixed default `apply_model_errors`
!     and `parse_mpc_obs_result` currently query with for every observatory
!     (see the per-site catalog-code TODOs in `model_correction.rs` and
!     `mpc.rs`) — a `c=*` entry would silently never be reached by either
!     call site, since "*" is only ever queried explicitly by VFCC17-style
!     lookups, not by the FCCT14-style cascade this file shares.
!   - The relatively high clipped fraction (23.9%) suggests a meaningful
!     share of `diaObjectId` associations are not clean single static
!     sources (blending, variability, mismatched crossmatch) over the ~1
!     year baseline; the RMS below reflects the population that remains
!     after robust rejection of those cases.
!   - Valid for the data collection period stated above; LSST's astrometric
!     pipeline is expected to improve over the survey's lifetime, so this
!     entry should be periodically re-derived from more recent data.
!
! REPRODUCIBILITY
!
! The `diaSource` fields listed under DATA SOURCE can be retrieved for any
! date range through the Fink broker's datatransfer service
! (https://lsst.fink-portal.org/, "Data Transfer" / API), which exports matching
! alerts as Parquet. To re-derive this entry from a fresh export covering a
! later period:
!
!   1. Request `diaSource.{diaSourceId, diaObjectId, ra, dec, raErr, decErr,
!      midpointMjdTai}` for the desired date range via datatransfer.
!   2. Drop rows with a null or non-positive `raErr`/`decErr`.
!   3. Group by `diaObjectId`, keep groups with >= 3 detections.
!   4. Per group and per axis (RA projected by cos(dec), Dec), fit the
!      constant- and linear-in-time models in closed form and select between
!      them with a one-degree-of-freedom F-test (alpha = 0.01, linear model
!      only considered for groups with >= 4 detections).
!   5. Compute per-point residuals against the selected model, apply an
!      iterative 5-sigma MAD-based clip across all retained residuals.
!   6. The RMS of the surviving residuals, per axis, is the new entry.
!
! ============================================================================

X05:  c=c    @ 0.09, 0.06
